Then Kent says:

+Food is eaten in haste, by an adventurer trying desperately to bolt it
+down before the next wandering monster comes by to dispute its
+possession with the adventurer. This is the model to keep in mind when
+trying to decide what is "realistic"

Are you saying that your typical Adventurer will have both cheeks full
like a chipmunk, and also be chewing?  Be serious.  Unless one is
compelled magically to eat, anyone will show *some* caution, knowing that
at any moment something nasty could come around the corner or an invisible
stalker that's *already* there could wack you.  In fact I might buy that:
"The invisisible stalker hits you while you're eating, you're so startled
you choke";-) ;-).

Someone full is less likely to bolt food down than someone "weak" or "fainting".

+More hasty still will be the adventurer trying to shovel down the
+immense corpse that is known to provide an exceptionally valuable
+intrinsic, after an awsome battle that probably awoke every monster on
+the level. Of _course_ choking is a possibility, and choking is nearly
+_guaranteed_ when one is trying to bolt food into a stomach whose gorge
+is rising in response due to already being satiated from recent similar
+behavior. The game is already _very_ realistic in how it treats eating.


I would by this case, on the condition that it's obvious to the character
that something unusual is at work.  I can't envision the exact message
but a distinction should be made between normal eating and ingesting
to gain in intristic.  I like the idea of "manna" that was posted.
How about "as you eat you feel the manna" when a character eats something
that will provide a missing intristic?  By evoking magic into the 
equation I would buy choking, you take your chances and mature characters
wouldn't die over "grazing" as they go thru the dungeon.

>it will always be a matter of contention about how to model eating
